n sampling ensures marginal distribution equals target. Or if using greedy? For stochastic sampling, distribution identical. Need mention target forward pass can evaluate multiple positions in parallel due to causal attention, so one pass yields logits for all draft positions. Speedup determined by acceptance rate, draft cost, target cost, batch size, hardware, sequence length, k, model sizes, memory/compute. In practice smaller due to low acceptance rate (draft mismatch, hard domains, high temperature, long context) and overhead/cost of draft model or verification (extra memory, latency, batch inefficiency, target pass not fully parallel, small k, synchronization). Need end with two most common reasons. Could be: (1) draft model often wrong, especially on hard/long/high-entropy text, causing rejections and wasted compute; (2) overhead of running draft and verifying (extra memory, latency, batch size, hardware) erases gains, especially when target pass is already compute-bound or batched. Need maybe mention if target is compute-bound, speculative decoding can be less effective? Actually for single sequence, target forward pass cost roughly independent of number of draft tokens (up to memory), so speedup if accepted. But in batched serving, adding draft tokens increases compute and may not help. Need two most common reasons: low acceptance and overhead/batching.\n\nNeed word count. Let's draft and count.\n\n\"Speculative decoding speeds up large language model inference by using a small draft model to propose", "top2": [[[1596, -0.001907438156194985], [22916, -6.797595977783203]], [[1144, -8.344684829353355e-06], [4087, -12.543850898742676]], [[4087, -0.24604375660419464], [310, -1.5314384698867798]], [[1156, -0.0019550349097698927], [303, -6.328464508056641]], [[579, -0.22323593497276306], [25, -1.8398970365524292]], [[1622, -0.009199606254696846], [3296, -4.890478610992432]], [[25, -0.3141644597053528], [13, -1.3300487995147705]], [[59987, -0.1397639662027359], [78